# Navigation policies

> Control which sites a session can navigate to, by category and by domain

A navigation policy decides which sites a browser session may load. You create a policy once, then bind it to sessions. The policy then allows or blocks each navigation in a bound session by its domain overrides, its category rules, and its default decision.

## How Browserbase decides a navigation

Browserbase checks each navigation's host in this order:

1. **Domain overrides.** If an override covers the host, its decision applies. An override for `example.com` also covers `www.example.com` and every other subdomain. When several overrides cover a host, the most specific one wins, so `allow` on `docs.example.com` beats `block` on `example.com`.
2. **Categories.** Otherwise, Browserbase classifies the host into one or more [categories](#categories). Each category takes its rule from `allowedCategories` or `blockedCategories`, or `defaultDecision` if the policy has no rule for it. If Browserbase can't classify the host, or part of its classification has no Browserbase category, that part counts as unknown and takes `unknownDomainBehavior`. The strictest result wins: if the policy blocks any of the host's categories, Browserbase blocks the navigation.

Because every category without a rule takes the default, a policy with `defaultDecision: "block"` only allows a host when the policy allows all of its categories. Add an `allow` override for sites you always need.

If Browserbase can't reach a decision in time, for example because classification is unavailable, it blocks the navigation. Overrides don't need classification, so they keep working.

## What a policy checks

A policy applies to **navigations**:

* page loads in the top frame, including links, form submissions, and `page.goto()`
* iframes
* popups and `window.open()`
* tabs opened with a URL, such as `Target.createTarget`
* each hop of a redirect

A policy doesn't check requests a page makes itself, such as scripts, images, `fetch` and XHR calls, and WebSockets. It also doesn't check pages that don't load from the network, such as `about:blank`, `data:`, and `blob:` URLs.

When the policy blocks a navigation:

* **Top frame:** the page lands on `https://www.browserbase.com/navigation-blocked`. `page.goto()` doesn't throw, so check `page.url()` to detect a block.
* **Iframe:** the frame fails to load with `net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT`. The rest of the page keeps loading.

### Service workers and Contexts

A site's service worker can answer a navigation without it ever reaching the network. In a policy-bound session that loads a [Context](https://docs.browserbase.com/platform/browser/core-features/contexts.md), pages skip their service workers, so the policy checks every navigation. Sites that depend on a service worker, such as for offline support, run without it in these sessions.

## Create a policy

Policies belong to the project of the API key that creates them. Fields you omit take these defaults: enabled, `defaultDecision: "block"`, `unknownDomainBehavior: "block"`, no category rules, and no overrides.

> [tab] Node.js
>
> ```typescript Node.js theme={null}
> import Browserbase from "@browserbasehq/sdk";
>
> const bb = new Browserbase({ apiKey: process.env.BROWSERBASE_API_KEY! });
>
> const policy = await bb.navigationPolicies.create({
>   name: "Research agents",
>   defaultDecision: "allow",
>   unknownDomainBehavior: "block",
>   blockedCategories: ["gambling", "adult", "malware", "phishing-scams"],
>   domainOverrides: [
>     { domain: "internal.example.com", decision: "block" },
>     { domain: "partner.example.net", decision: "allow" },
>   ],
> });
>
> console.log(policy.id);
> ```

> [tab] Python
>
> ```python Python theme={null}
> import os
>
> from browserbase import Browserbase
>
> bb = Browserbase(api_key=os.environ["BROWSERBASE_API_KEY"])
>
> policy = bb.navigation_policies.create(
>     name="Research agents",
>     default_decision="allow",
>     unknown_domain_behavior="block",
>     blocked_categories=[
>         "gambling",
>         "adult",
>         "malware",
>         "phishing-scams",
>     ],
>     domain_overrides=[
>         {
>             "domain": "internal.example.com",
>             "decision": "block",
>         },
>         {
>             "domain": "partner.example.net",
>             "decision": "allow",
>         },
>     ],
> )
>
> print(policy.id)
> ```

> [tab] cURL
>
> ```bash cURL theme={null}
> curl -X POST https://api.browserbase.com/v1/navigation-policies \
>   -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
>   -H "x-bb-api-key: $BROWSERBASE_API_KEY" \
>   -d '{
>     "name": "Research agents",
>     "defaultDecision": "allow",
>     "unknownDomainBehavior": "block",
>     "blockedCategories": ["gambling", "adult", "malware", "phishing-scams"],
>     "domainOverrides": [
>       { "domain": "internal.example.com", "decision": "block" },
>       { "domain": "partner.example.net", "decision": "allow" }
>     ]
>   }'
> ```

The response is the stored policy, with its `id`. Browserbase stores domains lowercase without a trailing dot.

| Field | Limits |
| - | - |
| `name` | 1 to 128 characters. Names don't need to be unique; a policy's `id` identifies it. |
| `description` | Up to 1024 characters. |
| `domainOverrides` | Up to 500. Each host may appear once. |
| `allowedCategories`, `blockedCategories` | Slugs from the [category list](#categories), each at most once. |

## Bind a policy to a session

Pass the policy's ID as `browserSettings.riskPolicyId` when you create a session. The policy must belong to the session's project and be enabled; otherwise session creation fails with `400`.

> [tab] Node.js
>
> ```typescript Node.js theme={null}
> import Browserbase from "@browserbasehq/sdk";
>
> const bb = new Browserbase({ apiKey: process.env.BROWSERBASE_API_KEY! });
>
> const session = await bb.sessions.create({
>   browserSettings: {
>     riskPolicyId: "<policy-id>",
>   },
> });
> ```

> [tab] Python
>
> ```python Python theme={null}
> import os
>
> from browserbase import Browserbase
>
> bb = Browserbase(api_key=os.environ["BROWSERBASE_API_KEY"])
>
> session = bb.sessions.create(
>     browser_settings={
>         "risk_policy_id": "<policy-id>",
>     },
> )
> ```

> [tab] cURL
>
> ```bash cURL theme={null}
> curl -X POST https://api.browserbase.com/v1/sessions \
>   -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
>   -H "x-bb-api-key: $BROWSERBASE_API_KEY" \
>   -d '{
>     "browserSettings": {
>       "riskPolicyId": "<policy-id>"
>     }
>   }'
> ```

A policy-bound session doesn't allow new browser contexts: `browser.newContext()` fails with `CDP command Target.createBrowserContext is not allowed`. In Playwright, open pages on the session's default context instead:

```typescript Node.js Playwright theme={null}
const browser = await chromium.connectOverCDP(session.connectUrl);
const context = browser.contexts()[0];
const page = context.pages()[0] ?? (await context.newPage());

await page.goto("https://example.com");
```

## Edit, disable, or delete a policy

A session keeps the policy as it was when the session started. Edits, disables, and deletes apply only to sessions that start afterwards.

* **Edit** with `PATCH /v1/navigation-policies/{id}`. Omitted fields keep their values. Lists such as `domainOverrides` replace the stored list rather than appending to it.
* **Disable** by setting `enabled` to `false`. New sessions can't bind a disabled policy. It stays editable, and you can re-enable it.
* **Delete** with `DELETE /v1/navigation-policies/{id}`.

See the [navigation policies API reference](https://docs.browserbase.com/reference/api/create-a-navigation-policy.md) for every route and field.

## Categories

| | | | |
| - | - | - | - |
| `adult` | `ai-tools` | `alcohol-tobacco` | `business` |
| `child-abuse` | `communication` | `dating` | `drugs` |
| `education` | `entertainment` | `finance` | `gambling` |
| `games` | `government-military` | `hacking` | `hate` |
| `health` | `illegal-activity` | `infrastructure` | `kids` |
| `lifestyle` | `malware` | `newly-registered` | `news` |
| `parked-inactive` | `phishing-scams` | `piracy` | `proxy-anonymizer` |
| `search-portal` | `self-harm` | `shopping` | `social-media` |
| `society-culture` | `sports` | `technology` | `terrorism-extremism` |
| `travel` | `url-shortener` | `violence` | `weapons` |

A host can belong to more than one category.

